.TH GITPULSE 1 "November 2026" "1.5.0" "GitPulseMLC Manual"
.SH NAME
gitpulse \- Monitor the heartbeat of local Git repositories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gitpulse
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B gitpulse add
[\fIADD-OPTIONS\fR] [\fIPATH\fR ...]
.br
.B gitpulse ignore
|
.B watch
\fINAME\fR|\fIPATH\fR ...
.br
.B gitpulse mark
\fINAME\fR|\fIPATH\fR ... \fBown\fR|\fB3rd\fR
.br
.B gitpulse mark \-\-auto
[\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
.br
.B gitpulse remove
\fINAME\fR|\fIPATH\fR ...
.br
.B gitpulse remove \-\-missing
[\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
.br
.B gitpulse list
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gitpulse
is a high-performance dashboard designed to monitor a large number of local Git repositories simultaneously. It provides a centralized view of your development environment, ensuring you never lose track of uncommitted changes, unpushed branches, or stale worktrees.
.PP
gitpulse only reads your repositories: it never commits, fetches, pulls or pushes. It writes only its own configuration (through the project commands) and a small cache. Its one network request is an anonymous HTTPS check whether a remote is public (see REMOTE VISIBILITY);
.B \-\-offline
skips it. Third-party projects and remotes on the local network are never checked.
.PP
.B Note for Windows Users:
Windows Defender or other antivirus tools might flag the generated binary as suspicious because it is unsigned and performs concurrent file system scans. If execution is blocked, you may need to add an exclusion for the binary or its directory.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-all
Enable verbose mode. Displays details for all remotes and submodules, all local branches, including ahead/behind counts relative to their tracking branches, and information about active stashes.
.TP
.B \-\-compact
Enable compact mode. Only repositories that require action (dirty state, ahead/behind commits, or stashes) will be displayed. Clean repositories are hidden.
.TP
.BI \-\-jobs " N"
Scan up to N repositories in parallel (default: one per CPU). Lower it on slow disks.
.TP
.B \-\-public
Only show repositories with a publicly readable remote.
.TP
.BI \-\-layout " auto|wide|narrow|stacked"
Table layout. auto (default) fits the terminal: wide shows every column, narrow drops PATH and shortens long branch names, stacked shows two lines per repository. A long status wraps within its column. Output that is not a terminal is always wide and never cut; $COLUMNS overrides the detected width.
.TP
.B \-\-offline
Do not check online whether remotes are public; use cached results only.
.TP
.B \-\-progress
Show a real-time scanning progress indicator on stderr. Only applicable in TUI mode.
.TP
.B \-\-html
Output results as a standalone, high-contrast HTML report. Useful for generating automated status emails.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Output results in JSON format for programmatic processing or data export.
.TP
.B \-\-no-color
Disable ANSI color output and use plain text. Also affects HTML output.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version information and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a summary of command-line options and exit.
.SH MANAGING PROJECTS
.B gitpulse add
(alias:
.BR init )
adds the repository at each \fIPATH\fR (default: the current directory), or every repository below \fIPATH\fR if it is a plain folder. If no configuration exists yet, it creates ~/.gitpulsemlc/repos.ini with default settings. Hidden directories, node_modules and vendor are skipped, and the search does not descend into a repository it has found. Projects already in the configuration, watched or ignored, are not added again. A repository whose remotes are not listed in own_remotes is added as third party.
.PP
.B gitpulse ignore
moves projects, given by name or path, to [disabled_projects]; add will not add them again. A repository not yet in the configuration is recorded as ignored.
.B gitpulse watch
watches ignored projects again.
.PP
.B gitpulse mark
\fINAME\fR ... \fBown\fR|\fB3rd\fR moves projects between [projects] and [third_party]. Third-party projects are listed in their own block; their local work is shown as hints and not counted as Dirty, Ahead, Behind or Public; only new upstream commits need action.
.B gitpulse mark \-\-auto
moves own projects whose remotes are not in own_remotes to third party (\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR only lists them).
.PP
.B gitpulse remove
(alias
.BR rm )
forgets projects; nothing on disk is touched. Unlike ignore, a later add of a parent folder finds them again.
.B \-\-missing
forgets every project whose folder is gone or holds no Git repository (\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR only lists them).
.PP
.B gitpulse list
prints every project with kind (own, 3rd) and state (watched, ignored, missing, dup).
.PP
Options of add:
.TP
.BI \-\-depth " N"
Number of directory levels to search (default 4).
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
List what would be added without writing anything.
.TP
.BR \-\-own ", " \-\-3rd
Add as your own or as third party, whatever the remote.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Configuration file to create or extend.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The tool looks for a configuration file named \fBrepos.ini\fR. It searches in the following order:
1. Path provided via \fB--config\fR flag.
2. Path in \fBGITPULSE_CONFIG\fR environment variable.
3. \fBconfig/repos.ini\fR in the current directory.
4. \fB~/.gitpulsemlc/repos.ini\fR in the user's home directory.
.PP
.B [projects]
.br
Your own projects: names mapped to absolute filesystem paths.
.br
Example: \fImy-app = /home/me/code/my-app\fR
.PP
.B [third_party]
.br
Other people's repositories, checked out to look at or test; same format.
.PP
.B [hosts]
.br
Other machines whose repositories join the overview: name = command that starts gitpulse there (e.g. ssh win11 wsl.exe \-d Ubuntu \-e /home/me/.local/bin/gitpulse). gitpulse appends \-\-json (and \-\-offline when given), runs all hosts in parallel and shows each as its own block; a host that fails or exceeds host_timeout appears as one ERROR line.
.PP
.B [disabled_projects]
.br
Ignored projects: not scanned, and add does not add them again.
.PP
Each project name must be unique across all sections; of a name that occurs twice only the last entry is used.
.PP
.B [general]
.br
\fBreplace_path_prefix\fR: A filesystem prefix to be replaced in the output (e.g., /home/me).
.br
\fBreplace_with\fR: The replacement string (e.g., "@").
.PP
.B [settings]
.br
\fBcolumn_name_width\fR: Minimum width for the project name column (default: 25).
.br
\fBshow_summary\fR: Boolean to toggle the final summary block (default: true).
.br
\fBcompact_mode\fR: Boolean to enable compact mode by default (default: false).
.br
\fBlayout\fR: Default for \-\-layout (default: auto).
.br
\fBstat_check\fR: full (default) compares tracked files like git status; minimal compares only size and modification time \- much faster on checkouts shared with Windows git, but line endings a Windows tool changed go unnoticed.
.br
\fBhost_timeout\fR: Seconds a host from [hosts] may take (default: 180).
.br
\fBown_remotes\fR: Comma-separated remotes that are yours, as host or host/owner (e.g. github.com/me); repositories elsewhere are added as third party.
.br
\fBprivate_hosts\fR: Comma-separated glob patterns of Git hosts that count as local network and are never checked online.
.SH REMOTE VISIBILITY
The REMOTE column shows who can read a repository:
.B pub
(anyone, without credentials),
.B priv
(external host that refused an anonymous read),
.B lan
(file path or host on the local network, never checked online),
.B ?
(unknown) or a dash (no remote). With several remotes the most open one is shown.
External hosts are checked with an anonymous HTTPS request to /info/refs?service=git-upload-pack, without Git configuration or credentials; SSH aliases are resolved with ssh \-G first.
Results are cached for 7 days in the user cache directory (gitpulse/visibility.json).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.gitpulsemlc/repos.ini
User configuration; created by gitpulse add.
.TP
.I config/repos.ini
Configuration in the current directory; takes precedence over the user configuration.
.TP
.I $XDG_CACHE_HOME/gitpulse/visibility.json
Cached visibility results (Linux: ~/.cache/..., macOS: ~/Library/Caches/...); safe to delete.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GITPULSE_CONFIG
Path of the configuration file, if \-\-config is not given.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A fatal error, e.g. no configuration, or a project command failed.
